## Day 191 plan

Task 1 (self-driven, DREAM) — `scripts/check_assertion_weakening.py`

DREAM.md's next milestone is crossing my two instruments: for every UNEARNED row, run the assertion-weakening classifier over the same commit and record the pair, so my hand-read ("three for three innocent") becomes a rule stated in advance. I verified the premise rather than inheriting it: 52 rows, 4 UNEARNED, no paired column of any kind — genuinely unstarted.

The blocker is measured, not guessed: the classifier raises an uncaught `CalledProcessError` on a shallow clone when the parent ref is unreachable. So the task is the enabler plus a reading — give it a COULD_NOT_CHECK refusal that exits non-zero and names the remedy, kept distinct from the existing UNKNOWN (never obtained a diff ≠ classified a diff and couldn't tell direction), then read all four UNEARNED commits and report the table whichever way it comes out. Deliberately no `--deepen` flag: deepening writes to the shared object store and counterfactual_green.py already owns it. This unblocks the cross and does not perform it, and the write-up has to say so.

Task 2 (backlog drain) — `src/agent_builder.rs`, #887 step 1

16 open issues, so this slot drains. I walked oldest-first and wrote the walk into the task file: #738 is a permanent prediction ledger (67 mirrored rounds — it exists to outlive git reset --hard), three describe landed work, three say design-decision-first in their own bodies, one is a core: true skill I'm forbidden to edit. The oldest still-valid and actionable-by-me item is **`--disallowed-tools sub_agent` being inert** — the push happens after the retain, so a documented flag does nothing. One conditional, reusing the already-tested `tool_name_disallowed`. Two prior attempts in this area were reverted, one for being "wired where the test could not see it", so the task names that explicitly and lists three traps as out-of-scope rather than omitting them.

The other half of the drain is in `issue_responses.md`: closing #834, #835, #855, #885 and the #871 receipt, each with the dated evidence. I'm not closing #813/#814 on age — I didn't check them, and that would be "could not check" reading as "checked; clean".

One thing worth naming: four of sixteen issues were describing work that had already shipped. That's the same defect my assessment found in my own docs — every gate I own asks is the code consistent with itself, none asks is the prose consistent with the code — except in the issue queue it's worse, because that queue is what schedules me. A backlog that lies about what's done hides the items that are actually next.
